Before Java Concurrency, You Must Understand Hardware
Most developers think concurrency starts here:

Thread t = new Thread();

It doesn't.

Concurrency actually starts inside the CPU.

Everything in Java concurrency is built to deal with the realities of modern processors.

Chapter 1 — Why Do CPUs Have Caches?
Imagine a CPU running at 4 GHz.

That means:

4,000,000,000 cycles/sec

RAM, however, is much slower.

Approximate access times:

Component	Latency
CPU Register	~1 cycle
L1 Cache	~4 cycles
L2 Cache	~12 cycles
L3 Cache	~40 cycles
Main Memory (RAM)	100–300+ cycles

If every variable read required going to RAM, the CPU would spend much of its time waiting.

So CPUs introduce caches.

CPU
 │
 │
L1 Cache
 │
L2 Cache
 │
L3 Cache
 │
RAM

The closer the memory is to the CPU, the faster it is—but also the smaller it is.

Why Is This Relevant to Java?
Suppose you have:

int counter = 10;

You might think the CPU reads from RAM every time.

It doesn't.

The CPU typically loads the value into its cache.

RAM

counter = 10

becomes

CPU Cache

counter = 10

Subsequent reads use the cache because it's much faster.

Now Introduce Two CPUs
Modern servers have multiple cores.

CPU 1

L1

L2

        Shared L3

L2

L1

CPU 2

Now both CPUs may have their own cached copy of the same variable.

RAM

counter = 10

CPU 1 cache

counter = 10

CPU 2 cache

counter = 10

Initially, everything is consistent.

Then One CPU Modifies It
CPU 1 executes:

counter++;

Its cache becomes:

CPU1 Cache

counter = 11

But CPU 2 may still have:

CPU2 Cache

counter = 10

This creates a visibility problem.

The question becomes:

How does CPU 2 learn about the update?

Modern processors solve this using cache coherence protocols (such as MESI), which coordinate cache updates and invalidations across cores. The Java Memory Model relies on the guarantees provided by the underlying hardware together with JVM-generated memory barriers.

This Is the First Real Concurrency Problem
It's not about Java.

It's about hardware.

One core has newer data.

Another core has stale data.

Java cannot ignore this.

The language specification must define rules so that multithreaded programs behave predictably.

That specification is the Java Memory Model (JMM).

The Second Problem — Compiler Optimizations
Suppose you write:

int x = a + b;
int y = c + d;

The compiler might decide to evaluate y before x if there is no dependency between them.

Similarly, the CPU can execute independent instructions out of order to keep its execution units busy.

This is called instruction reordering.

Example:

You write:

a = 1;
flag = true;

The compiler or CPU might effectively perform:

flag = true;
a = 1;

if nothing in the current thread depends on the original order.

For a single-threaded program, this is fine because the observable result is the same.

In multithreaded code, another thread may observe flag == true while still seeing the old value of a.

Now you have an ordering problem.

The Third Problem — Atomicity
Consider:

count++;

It looks like one operation.

It isn't.

It expands conceptually into:

Read count
↓

Increment value
↓

Write back

For example:

Read → 10

Increment → 11

Write → 11

If another thread performs the same sequence at the same time, one update can overwrite the other.

This is the classic lost update problem.

So count++ is not atomic.

At This Point We Have Three Fundamental Problems
Everything else in Java concurrency exists to solve one or more of these:

Problem	Cause	Solution Examples
Visibility	CPU caches	volatile, synchronized, locks
Ordering	Compiler/CPU reordering	Happens-before rules, memory barriers
Atomicity	Read-modify-write sequences	synchronized, CAS, atomic classes, locks

This table is worth memorizing because it explains the purpose of nearly every concurrency primitive in Java.

Why the Java Memory Model Exists
The Java Language Specification cannot simply say:

"Threads share memory."

That would be insufficient because CPUs, caches, compilers, and the JVM all perform optimizations.

Instead, the JMM defines:

When writes by one thread become visible to another.
Which reorderings are allowed.
Which synchronization actions establish ordering.
What guarantees volatile, synchronized, final fields, thread start/join, and other constructs provide.
Without the JMM, Java programs could behave differently on different CPU architectures.

The JMM provides a consistent contract across platforms.

