Chapter 1: The Journey of a Java Statement
Let's start with the simplest program.

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int x = 10;
        x++;
    }
}

Looks simple.

But when you run this, the following components participate:

Java Source Code
        │
        ▼
javac Compiler
        │
        ▼
Bytecode (.class)
        │
        ▼
Class Loader
        │
        ▼
JVM Runtime
        │
        ▼
JIT Compiler
        │
        ▼
Machine Code
        │
        ▼
CPU
        │
        ▼
Registers
        │
        ▼
ALU
        │
        ▼
Cache
        │
        ▼
RAM

Every concurrency concept we'll study later originates somewhere in this pipeline.

Step 1 — Java Source Code
When you write

int x = 10;

you're writing in a high-level language.

The compiler understands Java syntax.

The CPU does not.

The CPU understands only machine instructions such as:

MOV
ADD
SUB
CMP
JMP
LOAD
STORE

So Java source must first be translated.

Step 2 — javac Compiles to Bytecode
The Java compiler (javac) translates your code into bytecode.

Conceptually, your source:

int x = 10;
x++;

becomes bytecode similar to:

iconst_10
istore_1

iinc 1,1

What do these mean?

iconst_10

Push integer 10 onto the operand stack.

istore_1

Store that value into local variable slot 1.

iinc

Increment local variable 1 by 1.

Notice something interesting:

The JVM is stack-based, not register-based.

We'll come back to this distinction later because it influences bytecode design.

Question: Why Doesn't Java Compile Directly to Machine Code?
Because Java follows the Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA) principle.

If javac produced x86 machine code, it would only run on x86 processors.

Instead, Java compiles to platform-independent bytecode.

Later, each JVM translates that bytecode into native machine instructions for the current CPU architecture.

Java Source

↓

Bytecode

↓

Windows JVM → x86

Linux JVM → x86

Mac JVM → ARM

Android Runtime → ARM

Same .class file.

Different machine code.

Step 3 — Class Loading
Suppose you execute:

java Main

The JVM doesn't immediately execute your program.

First it asks:

"Where is Main.class?"

The Class Loader Subsystem loads it into memory.

This involves several phases:

Loading

↓

Linking

↓

Initialization

We'll study these deeply in the JVM module, but for now:

Loading
Reads the .class file.

Linking
Verification
Preparation
Resolution
Initialization
Runs static initialization.

For example:

class Test {

    static {

        System.out.println("Loaded");
    }
}

The static block executes during initialization.

Step 4 — The JVM Interpreter
Most developers think Java is immediately JIT compiled.

That's not true.

Initially, the JVM interprets bytecode.

Bytecode

↓

Interpreter

↓

Execute instruction

↓

Next instruction

↓

Execute instruction

For our example:

iconst_10

↓

Execute

↓

istore

↓

Execute

↓

iinc

↓

Execute

This interpretation is slower than native execution.

So why interpret first?

Because the JVM doesn't yet know whether the method is worth compiling.

Step 5 — Profiling
Suppose you have:

for(int i=0;i<100000000;i++){

    sum+=i;

}

The JVM notices:

Method executed

↓

100 times

↓

1000 times

↓

10000 times

↓

50000 times

At some threshold (which depends on JVM heuristics), the method is considered hot.

A hot method is a good candidate for optimization.

Step 6 — JIT Compilation
Now the Just-In-Time (JIT) Compiler takes over.

Bytecode

↓

Machine Code

↓

Store in Code Cache

↓

Execute directly

From this point, execution bypasses the interpreter and runs native instructions.

This is one reason Java can approach the performance of C/C++ in long-running applications.

Two JIT Compilers
Modern HotSpot has two main JIT compilers:

C1 (Client Compiler)
Fast compilation
Moderate optimizations
Used early in execution
C2 (Server Compiler)
Slower compilation
Aggressive optimizations
Produces highly optimized code
Conceptually:

Interpreter

↓

C1

↓

Profile More

↓

C2

This staged approach balances startup time with peak performance.

Step 7 — Machine Code
Suppose the JIT generates x86-64 machine code.

Conceptually, x++ might become something like:

mov eax,10

add eax,1

or

inc eax

The exact instructions depend on optimization and surrounding code, but the important point is that the JVM has translated bytecode into the processor's native instruction set.

Now the CPU can execute it.

Step 8 — CPU Fetch-Decode-Execute Cycle
Every CPU continuously repeats this cycle:

Fetch

↓

Decode

↓

Execute

↓

Fetch

↓

Decode

↓

Execute

Fetch
Read the next machine instruction from memory (typically via the instruction cache).

Decode
Determine what operation the instruction represents.

Execute
Perform the operation.

This loop runs billions of times per second.

Step 9 — Registers
Most developers imagine variables live in RAM.

Not while they're actively being used.

Consider:

int x = 10;
x++;

The JIT will often keep x entirely in a CPU register.

CPU

Register R1 = 10

Then:

INC R1

Result:

Register R1 = 11

No RAM access is needed for this simple case.

Registers are the fastest storage in the system, and the JIT works hard to keep frequently used values there.

Why Registers Matter for Concurrency
Imagine two threads.

Core 1

Register
x = 10

Core 2

Register
x = 10

This isn't possible for the same local variable because each thread has its own stack and local variables.

However, shared object fields are different. They may be loaded into each core's registers or caches independently during execution.

This distinction is crucial:

Local variables (method parameters and local primitives/references) are thread-confined and generally don't require synchronization.
Shared heap data (instance fields, static fields, array elements) can be observed by multiple threads and are where concurrency problems arise.
Step 10 — Where Is x Stored?
This is a question that often causes confusion.

Consider:

public void test() {

    int x = 10;
}

x is a local variable.

Its lifecycle is roughly:

Java Local Variable

↓

JVM Local Variable Slot

↓

JIT Optimization

↓

CPU Register

It may never touch the heap at all.

Now compare:

class Employee {

    int salary;
}

Here, salary is an instance field.

Employee Object

↓

Heap

↓

Cache

↓

CPU Register (when accessed)

Concurrency is primarily concerned with shared heap fields, not local variables.

Key Takeaways
Java source is compiled to platform-independent bytecode.
The JVM initially interprets bytecode.
Frequently executed ("hot") methods are JIT compiled into native machine code.
The CPU executes machine instructions, not Java code.
Frequently used values are often kept in CPU registers, avoiding memory access.
Local variables are thread-confined; shared heap data is where visibility and synchronization become relevant.


Lesson 2 — CPU Architecture: The Foundation of Java Concurrency
Goal: By the end of this lesson, you should understand how a CPU is organized internally and why its design directly leads to the existence of the Java Memory Model (JMM), volatile, synchronized, CAS, and locks.

Before We Begin
Most Java developers think a CPU is just a "processor."

In reality, a modern CPU is an incredibly sophisticated machine containing:

Billions of transistors
Multiple cores
Multiple cache levels
Pipelines
Branch predictors
Out-of-order execution engines
Register files
Load/Store units
Execution units
Every Java statement eventually becomes electrical signals moving through these components.

Everything in Java concurrency exists because of how this hardware works.

Chapter 1 — What Exactly Is a CPU?
Imagine you're the CEO of a company.

You don't personally fetch documents from storage, perform calculations, or keep records. Instead:

You read instructions
You make decisions
Others retrieve data
Others perform calculations
A CPU works similarly.

It doesn't "know Java."

It only understands binary instructions like:

LOAD
STORE
MOV
ADD
SUB
MUL
DIV
JMP
CMP

Everything—from Spring Boot to Kafka to the JVM—is eventually reduced to these primitive operations.

CPU Internal Organization
A simplified CPU looks like this:

                    CPU

        +------------------------+
        |                        |
        |   Control Unit (CU)    |
        |                        |
        +------------------------+
                  |
                  |
        +------------------------+
        |                        |
        | Arithmetic Logic Unit  |
        |         (ALU)          |
        |                        |
        +------------------------+
                  |
                  |
        +------------------------+
        |                        |
        |      Registers         |
        |                        |
        +------------------------+
                  |
                  |
        +------------------------+
        |                        |
        |   L1 Cache             |
        |                        |
        +------------------------+

These are the essential components we'll explore.

Chapter 2 — Registers
Registers are the fastest storage available in a computer.

Approximate access times:

Storage	Relative Speed
Registers	Fastest
L1 Cache	Very Fast
L2 Cache	Fast
L3 Cache	Moderate
RAM	Much Slower
SSD	Far Slower

A register is simply a tiny storage location inside the CPU.

Example:

int x = 10;

After JIT compilation, conceptually:

Register R1 = 10

When executing:

x++;

The CPU may execute:

INC R1

Result:

Register R1 = 11

Notice something important.

No RAM access occurred.

The CPU prefers registers because accessing RAM is expensive.

Why Not Store Everything in Registers?
Because there are very few of them.

Modern x86 CPUs expose a limited set of general-purpose registers, for example:

RAX
RBX
RCX
RDX
RSI
RDI
RBP
RSP
...

The compiler and JIT decide which values deserve a register.

If there aren't enough registers, values are "spilled" to memory.

This optimization is called register allocation.

Chapter 3 — Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)
The ALU is the CPU's calculator.

It performs operations like:

5 + 3

10 - 2

4 * 7

10 > 3

AND

OR

XOR

Suppose:

int c = a + b;

Conceptually:

Register1 = a

Register2 = b

↓

ALU

↓

Register3 = a+b

The ALU doesn't know anything about variables.

It only receives binary values from registers.

Chapter 4 — Control Unit (CU)
Think of the Control Unit as the conductor of an orchestra.

It coordinates everything.

Responsibilities include:

Fetch the next instruction
Decode it
Send data to the ALU
Decide where the result goes
Update the Program Counter
Handle branches
Without the Control Unit, the CPU wouldn't know what to execute next.

Chapter 5 — The Fetch–Decode–Execute Cycle
Every CPU repeats the same cycle billions of times per second.

Fetch

↓

Decode

↓

Execute

↓

Repeat

Let's examine this in detail.

Step 1 — Fetch
Suppose the machine code contains:

ADD RAX, RBX

The CPU fetches this instruction from memory (typically the instruction cache).

Memory

↓

CPU

Step 2 — Decode
The CPU asks:

"What operation is this?"

Answer:

ADD

The decoder determines:

Operation = ADD
Source Register = RBX
Destination Register = RAX
Step 3 — Execute
The ALU performs:

RAX + RBX

Stores the result back into:

RAX

Then the CPU moves on to the next instruction.

Program Counter (Instruction Pointer)
You already studied the JVM PC Register.

Now let's discuss the CPU's Program Counter.

It stores:

Address of next instruction

Example:

Instruction 100

↓

Instruction 104

↓

Instruction 108

The Program Counter advances after each instruction.

If there's an if statement:

if(a>b)

The Program Counter may jump to another location.

Hence the name:

Program Counter

CPU Core
Modern processors don't have just one execution unit.

Instead:

CPU

Core 1

Core 2

Core 3

Core 4

...

Each core has:

Registers
ALU
Pipelines
L1 Cache
L2 Cache
Often, the cores share an L3 cache.

Each core can execute instructions independently.

This is where Java threads begin to matter.

Mapping Java Threads to CPU Cores
Suppose you have:

Thread t1;

Thread t2;

The JVM asks the operating system to create native threads.

The operating system scheduler decides:

Core1 → Thread1

Core2 → Thread2

Now both cores execute simultaneously.

This is parallel execution.

First Important Observation
Imagine both threads access:

employee.salary

Both CPU cores load the value.

Heap

salary = 100

Core 1:

Register

salary = 100

Core 2:

Register

salary = 100

Initially, everything is consistent.

Now Core 1 updates it:

salary = 200

Core 2 may still hold:

salary = 100

Why?

Because registers and caches are local to a core.

This is our first glimpse of the visibility problem. The CPU is behaving correctly from a hardware perspective, but another core may continue using an older copy until the architecture's cache coherence mechanisms propagate the change.

Chapter 6 — CPU Pipelines
Imagine a washing machine.

You could:

Wash Shirt A

↓

Dry Shirt A

↓

Fold Shirt A

↓

Wash Shirt B

Very slow.

Instead:

Wash A

Wash B

Dry A

