Chapter 6 — Why Caches Are Small
You may ask:

"Why not make L1 cache 64 GB?"

Because faster memory is:

physically larger per bit,
more power-hungry,
more expensive,
and harder to keep close to the execution units.
L1 must sit extremely close to the CPU's execution pipelines.

That's why it's typically only tens of KB per core.

Chapter 7 — Data Moves in Cache Lines
This is one of the most important concepts in computer architecture.

Suppose you request:

emp.salary

Does the CPU load:

4 bytes?

No.

It loads an entire cache line.

On most modern CPUs:

64 bytes

So instead of loading:

salary

It loads:

------------------------------------------------
salary

age

id

department

bonus

managerId

...

64 Bytes
------------------------------------------------

The CPU assumes:

"If you accessed one field, you'll probably access nearby fields."

This is called:

Spatial Locality

Two Types of Locality
1. Temporal Locality
If you use something once,

you're likely to use it again soon.

Example:

sum += salary;

sum += salary;

sum += salary;

Same value repeatedly.

2. Spatial Locality
If you use one memory location,

you're likely to use nearby locations.

Example:

employee.id;

employee.name;

employee.salary;

These fields are typically located close together in memory.

