Instead, the CPU often performs the updates locally and later ensures the changes become visible according to the hardware's cache coherence rules and any synchronization requirements imposed by the programming model.

This optimization is great for performance—but it introduces complexity for concurrent programs.

Chapter 11 — Shared Variables vs Local Variables
This distinction is critical.

Local Variable
void calculate() {

    int x = 10;

}

x belongs only to the current thread.

No other thread can access it directly.

No synchronization needed.

Shared Variable
class Employee {

    int salary;

}

Two threads can execute:

employee.salary++;

Now both CPUs may cache the same field independently.

Synchronization may be required.

Chapter 12 — The Root of the Visibility Problem
Let's summarize.

Thread A

↓

CPU Core 1

↓

L1 Cache

↓

Updates salary

Thread B:

↓

CPU Core 2

↓

Different L1 Cache

↓

Reads old salary

Both threads believe they're correct.

The hardware needs rules to coordinate these copies.

Without those rules, programs would behave unpredictably.

This is exactly why modern processors implement cache coherence protocols.

Why This Matters for Java
Consider this loop:

while (!flag) {
    // wait
}

Another thread executes:

flag = true;

If the waiting thread keeps reading a stale cached value of flag, it may loop forever.

The Java Memory Model defines when changes made by one thread must become visible to another, and constructs like volatile and synchronized are how Java programmers express those requirements.

Summary
Today we've established several key ideas:

CPU cores are much faster than RAM, so they rely on caches.
Caches hold copies of data, not the authoritative version.
Data moves between memory and caches in cache lines (commonly 64 bytes).
Cache hits are fast; cache misses are expensive.
Each CPU core has its own private caches, so different cores can temporarily hold different copies of the same shared data.
This temporary divergence is the fundamental reason visibility problems exist in multithreaded programs.


