Lesson 3.2 — Why Were Threads Invented?
This is where many developers have the wrong mental model.

Most people think:

Threads exist to make programs faster.

That is only partially true.

The real reason is:

Threads exist because CPUs spend a lot of time waiting.

Let's understand why.

Imagine There Are No Threads
Suppose your application does this:

readFromDatabase();

calculateSalary();

writeToFile();

Timeline:

Database

↓

Calculation

↓

File Write

Looks fine.

But databases are slow compared to CPUs.

CPU Speed vs External Devices
Approximate latencies:

Operation	Typical Latency
CPU register access	<1 ns
L1 cache	~1 ns
L2 cache	~4 ns
Main memory (RAM)	~100 ns
SSD read	tens to hundreds of µs
Network request	ms to 100s of ms

The CPU can execute millions of instructions while waiting for a disk or network operation.

CPU Waiting Problem
Suppose:

Database request

↓

Wait 10 ms

The CPU isn't doing useful work during that wait for this execution path.

If the entire process has only one thread, nothing else in that process can make progress until the thread continues.

The CPU could run other processes, but your application itself isn't utilizing that waiting time.

The Better Idea
Instead of:

One execution path

↓

Wait

↓

Continue

Use multiple execution paths:

Thread A

↓

Waiting for Database

--------------------

Thread B

↓

Calculate Report

--------------------

Thread C

↓

Process Orders

While one thread waits, another thread in the same process can execute.

This improves responsiveness and throughput for many workloads, especially those involving I/O.

Why Threads Share Memory
Imagine if each thread had its own heap.

Thread A Heap

Employee

-----------------

Thread B Heap

Employee

Suppose Thread A updates:

employee.setSalary(100000);

Thread B still sees:

50000

That would make collaboration inside a single application extremely difficult.

Instead:

Shared Heap

Employee

Every thread can access the same object.

This design enables efficient communication, but it also introduces the concurrency problems we'll study later.

The Beginning of Concurrency Problems
Imagine:

Thread A

employee.salary = 100

--------------------

Thread B

employee.salary = 200

Both access the same memory.

Now important questions arise:

Which value should be visible?
What if they update at the same time?
Can one thread see stale data?
Can writes happen out of order?
Can the CPU or compiler reorder operations?
These are not Java-specific questions. They arise because multiple execution contexts share memory.

This is exactly why the rest of our roadmap exists:

